#{[17592186523910 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523910 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523910 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523910 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523910 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea67d905-4e13-5fb0-9a4b-c810c57cacc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523924 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/22/2242cf493c6307d8c6f2dac778efe6f89ca4ba6f04203940076360613c8dc9b0.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ support pole leans against a vertical wall, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. The base of the pole $B$ is being pulled toward the wall at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ At what rate is $\\theta $ increasing at the moment when the top of the pole $T$ is $5\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "017bbabc-f897-558b-b361-4f91d645fa28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523924 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/e0/e08cddcde0d709ebe455b8d8935174955798b877f054dab0de74d06035accc91.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the base of the pole to the wall, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the pole and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the pole. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the pole's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=5$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-2$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{13} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523931 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/ce513880934a99b289c22f734e8a455c3534bddd911968cd06b7fe88f65441aa.png)\n\nA hot air balloon $B$ is flying horizontally to the observer $O$ at a constant height of $300\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the balloon and an observer is increasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the balloon when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14bd2170-88ec-5846-a2ce-ea9d86c753dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523931 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/81/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523948 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a5/a51d0d8ef1be2e7fc06aa34fe1440e77a79d8bee654703c1e931332390741c32.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ ladder leans against the side of a building, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. Given that the foot of the ladder $F$ is being pulled away from the building at the rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/s}$, at what rate is $\\theta $ decreasing at the moment when the top of the ladder $T$ is $12\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "419bbacd-9dab-5629-96fc-19c329c4d054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523948 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/7b/7b923b76e90d665a722643684ce94fff50500a7be8b70aa821410a7969e4bb79.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the foot of the ladder to the wall of the building, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the ladder. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the ladder's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=12$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{26} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\cdot \\frac{13}{12}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\theta $ is *decreasing* at a rate of $\\frac{1}{24}$ radians per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523956 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8f4638863dbc6bdfbff00d20d2153bb73eb7779719bbc7235d730539cb92329e.png)\n\nAngela observes that a drone is flying horizontally at a constant height of $5\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. If the drone is flying in the opposite direction from Angela's location at a speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ decreases at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "579cea6b-f87a-550f-8093-6a43bd1625a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523956 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/80/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523958 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bf/bf0aa0508025b800bb0707b74ffdc89b08052ad6fced401b8071d7ad741f8e0a.png)\n\nA lighthouse $L$ shines a beam that hits the surface of the sea at point $B.$ The light is mounted at a height of $10\\,\\text{m}$ above the water. As the beam is tilted downward, the angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the beam and the point $B$ is increasing at a rate of $0.12$ radians per second. What is the speed of the light spot as a function of $\\theta ?$ \n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\nThe speed of the light spot is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a9f47ed-553f-5a71-99da-2ec914b9fd73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523958 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/bd/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523963 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/22/2264aa2a4f3d33b04190608ab638604dbe09dfd278035b2b764aa3d85d947624.png)\n\nGreta is flying a kite $K.$ The kite is  $150\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and moving horizontally towards Greta at a speed of $10\\,\\text{in/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bda9e93-d670-52bc-bf28-dd5b8dfcba8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523963 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/43/430d9bccfa7d9063ef3be511b894f23227fc248eb6afde9a25dc78546dde32aa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between Greta and the kite. The quantity $y=150$ inches does not change with time because the kite height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{150}{x} \\\\ x & =150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-10.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing is $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}$ radians per second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523964 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/06/0640883625711f20e1441946461abd0f352ed1418065b325f48ea2b709fef230.png)\n\nA cableway system is descending at an angle of ${45}^{∘},$ and the speed of its cabin $C$ is $10\\,\\text{mi/h}.$ The cabin casts a shadow $S$ vertically downwards to the ground. Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, find the rate at which the distance between the cableway's shadow to the landing point $L$ is decreasing." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7145361b-50f5-5e70-a538-4868d130565b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523964 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/4d/4d2a9ab6bc60aa5733be4dc29aea2b03d16edbebed371963b795297a4c79a676.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance from the cableway's shadow to the landing point, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the cabin, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,\\text{mi/h}$ (negative because the distance between the cableway's shadow and the landing point is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-10)=-5\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal distance is decreasing at a rate of $5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523976 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/55/55e96238e8263a1744c117bad09d1b11947f70a8bf1b837c52af18cf2e244ab7.png)\n\nA park ranger $P$ stands $900\\,\\text{ft}$ away from the base of a lookout tower $T$ to track a drone $D$ that is ascending vertically. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the ranger and the drone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ radians per second. At what rate is the drone gaining altitude at the moment when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone’s altitude is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82671b33-6326-52d6-9a89-56b07cb04dfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523976 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/d3/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523980 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d2e2666baf2931dc4e0d8ba097a364b7b7a1bba75e65c8d802a60180e9a94854.png)\n\nNeah is flying a kite $K$ with a string of length $112\\,\\text{ft}.$ The angle of elevation $\\theta $ of the kite is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{28}$ radians per second. Find, as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the kite's altitude is increasing." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8512c354-4d05-539a-b5eb-ac69014916bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523980 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/8c/8c3c9e40f56d0b269562c37fd2d87c7e8f0394c8f55dca596e5e751f5e96157b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Neah. The quantity $z=112\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{112} \\\\ y & =112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{28}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\pi }{28} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the kite is gaining altitude is $4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524009 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5e/5e96676aca66b99fd1e080d6c0cbbe79a5197125a3bcc8f04e67ca406947af10.png)\n\nA bullet $B$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ from the horizontal. Given that the bullet's horizontal speed is $300\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ calculate the actual speed of the bullet in the direction it is traveling." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c70a238b-9801-5892-a5eb-17f62e1925f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524009 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/fe/fea05a36487813579d4633d9134bdd1d8c4d4bef4e9f57ba997b7b8715d46f32.png)\n\nLet $x$ denote the horizontal distance from the bullet's projection to the cannon, and $z$ be the distance between the bullet and the cannon, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=300\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2\\cdot 300=600.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the plane is $600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524027 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d4/d486581ea48bc96adc1269fbf40d1d71a547e92977f71e3a74336a147ef9b222.png)\n\nA potato $P$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians from the horizontal. The horizontal distance the projectile travels increases at a rate of $18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ If the angle of elevation does not change, what is the speed of the potato?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e817aceb-adb1-5216-bfe1-e48f45d3b767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524027 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/8b/8b5af04f58fe65b46adaae47d97b857d1e8765122a811179cf25ba22733e6415.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the horizontal distance from the potato to the cannon and $z$ be the actual distance between them, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 18=12\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the potato is $12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524028 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6a/6a37ae024b0472dfc38ecff01b898d8d3ed2384d90acf432d747efee1568520e.png)\n\nA car $C$ drives toward a $108\\,\\text{m}$ high building $B$ following a straight path. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the car and the top of the building $T$ is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ radians per second. What is the speed of the car when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9479f5b-c4df-5efe-97d6-27688a7060c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524028 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/f2/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi (\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524031 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524031 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524031 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524031 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/30/305398b239bbc784fb5e5a1ec7ef2020a19a033dcda150e84f9c4a2a72a96adf.png)\n\nA lifeguard $L$ is watching a weather balloon $W$ rise vertically from a point $S$ on the beach. The point $S$ is $10\\,\\text{m}$ away from the lifeguard. If the balloon is rising at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the lifeguard and the balloon is increasing at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edce8cb0-3424-5fe2-ac49-b2f607c023b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524031 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/b4/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524038 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524038 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/96/96b70c61e20b693a29a125b5f03243a75d3bfd0aeb051a2f6f74525cfdb3da78.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and flies at a constant angle of ${45}^{∘}$ to the horizontal. How fast is the drone gaining altitude if its speed is $42\\,\\text{km/h}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f624216d-33b0-5bec-b567-6dca0950cf8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524038 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/40/40e0ae889d241c45464492ba3842d586effe528ab93b3554f6dbfb01d215c308.png)\n\nLet $y$ be the drone's altitude and $z$ be the actual distance between the drone and its take-off point, as shown below.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=42\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 42=21\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
